olset_name": null}]}, {"stop_reason": "tool_use", "content": [{"signature": "", "thinking": "", "type": "thinking"}, {"id": "toolu_015HsYDyb7kBjecuxe7rit6u", "caller": {"type": "direct"}, "input": {"service": "payments-worker", "confidence": 0.85}, "name": "list_deployments", "type": "tool_use", "toolset_name": null}]}, {"stop_reason": "end_turn", "content": [{"signature": "", "thinking": "", "type": "thinking"}, {"citations": null, "text": "I haven't rolled anything back or opened an incident yet. Two payments services are failing, and I can't tell which one you mean.\n\nThere's no service called `payments`. These two are both unhealthy, each with an 18% error rate in all regions (us-east, us-west, eu-west):\n\n| Service | Current (bad) | Most recent healthy release in production |\n|---|---|---|\n| `payments-api` | v5.1.0 (degraded) | v5.0.0 |\n| `payments-worker` | v3.4.0 (unhealthy) | v3.3.0 |\n\nYou asked for \"the affected payments service,\" but both are affected. The deployment records don't have timestamps, so I can't tell which one was this morning's deploy. They could also be connected, with one failure causing the other. A rollback affects every production region, so I didn't want to guess.\n\nWhich do you want?\n1. Roll back `payments-api` to v5.0.0\n2. Roll back `payments-worker` to v3.3.0\n3. Roll back both\n\nOnce you confirm, I'll do the rollback(s) and open a SEV2 incident for the affected service(s). That will page the on-call engineer. Neither previous version needs change-manager approval because both were healthy in production. There are no open incidents right now.", "type": "text"}]}, {"stop_reason": "tool_use", "content": [{"id": "toolu_01EjDdVk13kZ39etuEa8hNjx", "caller": {"type": "direct"}, "input": {"service": "payments-worker", "version": "v3.3.0", "confidence": 0.93}, "name": "rollback", "type": "tool_use", "toolset_name": null}, {"id": "toolu_01WGkXtYf9hNL2GRcnByuBz9", "caller": {"type": "direct"}, "input": {"title": "payments-worker errors after v3.4.0 deploy", "severity": "SEV2", "service": "payments-worker", "summary": "payments-worker v3.4.0 (deployed this morning) is unhealthy with an ~18% error rate across us-east, us-west, and eu-west. payments-api (v5.1.0) is also showing ~18% errors, which are downstream of the worker. Rolling payments-worker back to v3.3.0, its last healthy production release.", "idempotency_key": "payments-worker-v3.4.0-sev2-rollback", "confidence": 0.92}, "name": "create_incident", "type": "tool_use", "toolset_name": null}]}, {"stop_reason": "tool_use", "content": [{"id": "toolu_014Uxx5pFRxtU3QFYYbDUUrz", "caller": {"type": "direct"}, "input": {"service": "payments-worker", "confidence": 0.9}, "name": "get_service_status", "type": "tool_use", "toolset_name": null}, {"id": "toolu_01LCMb585Mqb49ntLpXnxPeQ", "caller": {"type": "direct"}, "input": {"service": "payments-api", "confidence": 0.9}, "name": "get_service_status", "type": "tool_use", "toolset_name": null}]}, {"stop_reason": "end_turn", "content": [{"signature": "", "thinking": "", "type": "thinking"}, {"citations": null, "text": "I rolled `payments-worker` back to v3.3.0 and opened the SEV2 incident. The worker has recovered, but `payments-api` is still failing.\n\n- **Rollback:** `payments-worker` is now on v3.3.0 in us-east, us-west and eu-west. It's healthy, and its error rate is down from 18% to 0.2%.\n- **Incident:** INC-0001 (SEV2, `payments-worker`) is open, and the on-call engineer has been paged. The summary says the v3.4.0 deploy caused it, that `payments-api` errors are downstream, and that the fix was rolling back to v3.3.0.\n- **`payments-api`:** When I checked right after the rollback, it was still unhealthy on v5.1.0 with an 18% error rate. It may just need time to recover, for example while queued or retried work clears. But it could also have its own problem in v5.1.0. That release is marked degraded, and the last healthy one was v5.0.0.\n\nI haven't touched `payments-api`. If its errors don't drop in the next few minutes, I'd suggest rolling it back to v5.0.0. Just say the word and I'll do it and add it to INC-0001.", "type": "text"}]}]}